\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
The security of a great deal of deployed public-key cryptography rests on the
presumed hardness of factoring a semiprime $N = pq$. Among proposed attacks,
the \emph{statistical} family has a persistent appeal: one computes a cheap,
factor-oblivious statistic of $N$, observes a bias, and asks whether the bias
depends on $p$ and $q$ separately in a way that could be inverted.

Two of the most classical statistics attached to a modulus are the
\emph{multiplicative order} and the \emph{Jacobi symbol}, and they are
complementary in a suggestive way:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item the Jacobi symbol $J(b\mid N)$ is computable in $O(\log^2 N)$ time by
  quadratic reciprocity, \emph{without knowing $p$ and $q$};
  \item the multiplicative order $\ord_N(b)$ is, for general composite $N$,
  believed to be as hard as factoring; order-finding is the core of Shor's
  algorithm.
\end{itemize}
If the joint distribution of the cheap statistic and the expensive one carried
information about $p$ and $q$ individually, that would be a genuine leak. This
paper determines the joint distribution exactly and shows that it does not.

\subsection{The object of study}

\begin{definition}[Joint law]
For an odd integer $N > 1$, the \emph{order $\times$ Jacobi joint law} of $N$ is
the multiset
\[
  \Lw(N) \;=\; \big\{\!\!\big\{\,\big(\ord_N(b),\; J(b\mid N)\big) \;:\; b \in \units{N} \,\big\}\!\!\big\}.
\]
\end{definition}

This is the \emph{maximal} statistic in its class: every conditional law
$\ord \mid J = \pm 1$, every conditional moment, quantile and entropy of the
pair is a function of $\Lw(N)$. A barrier stated at the level of $\Lw$
therefore rules out an entire family of attacks at once, not merely the
particular statistics that have been tried.

\subsection{Summary of results}
Throughout, $p \ne q$ are odd primes, $N = pq$,
\[
  H_p := \frac{p-1}{2}, \qquad H_q := \frac{q-1}{2}, \qquad L := \lcmop(H_p, H_q),
\]
and $v_2(m)$ denotes the exponent of $2$ in $m$. The results are: an exact
coupling at a prime (Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling}); the forward lift
(Theorem~\ref{thm:forward}); failure of the converse
(Theorem~\ref{thm:counterexample}); the $2$-adic dichotomy
(Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}); equidistribution of quadrants
(Theorems~\ref{thm:quadrants} and \ref{thm:orderclass}); blindness of the symbol
(Theorem~\ref{thm:blind}); the collision (Theorem~\ref{thm:collision}); the
barrier (Theorems~\ref{thm:abstractbarrier} and \ref{thm:concretebarrier}); and
transport (Theorem~\ref{thm:transport}).

\subsection{Relation to the empirical picture}
These results were reached in dialogue with a computational survey: $14$ primes
exhaustively, and $30$ near-equal-size semiprimes of magnitude about
$5\times 10^{6}$ with $1500$ sampled units each. Three empirical findings
emerged, and each is now explained.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
  \item The residue--order coupling verified on all $7000$ tested instances;
  Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling} shows it is an exact equivalence, so there is no
  statistical content to explain.
  \item The conditional bias
  $\Ex[\ord \mid J{=}{+}1]/\Ex[\ord \mid J{=}{-}1] \in [0.68, 1.01]$ is
  genuinely non-unit; Corollary~\ref{cor:bias} shows this is forced by the coset
  structure.
  \item Observed correlations of the conditional means and their ratio with
  $p$, $q$, $p+q$ and $|p-q|$ never exceeded $0.31$, against permutation-null
  $95$th percentiles of $0.34$--$0.41$; Theorems~\ref{thm:dichotomy},
  \ref{thm:collision} and \ref{thm:transport} explain why: the only structure
  the law carries is a $2$-adic dial, a function of $N \bmod 4$, and beyond
  that the law is many-to-one on moduli.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Notation and preliminaries}

For an odd prime $p$, the group $\units{p}$ is cyclic of order $p-1$. We write
$H_p = (p-1)/2$ for the exponent of its unique index-$2$ subgroup, the
\emph{half group}
$\mathcal{H}_p = \{ u : \ord_p(u) \mid H_p \}$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:H}
For odd $p$ we have $2H_p = p-1$; moreover $H_p$ is odd if and only if
$p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$, and $2 \mid H_p$ if and only if $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$.
Consequently
\begin{equation}\label{eq:v2dial}
  v_2(H_p) = 0 \iff p \equiv 3 \!\!\pmod 4, \qquad
  v_2(H_p) \ge 1 \iff p \equiv 1 \!\!\pmod 4 .
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $p = 2k+1$, so $H_p = k$; then $k$ is odd exactly when $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.
\end{proof}

For $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ odd primes, the Chinese Remainder Theorem gives a
group isomorphism
\begin{equation}\label{eq:crt}
  \pi : \units{N} \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ } \units{p} \times \units{q}, \qquad
  \pi(b) = (b \bmod p,\; b \bmod q).
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Order via components]\label{lem:ordlcm}
For $\gcd(p,q) = 1$ and any unit $b$ modulo $N = pq$,
$\ord_N(b) = \lcmop\!\big(\ord_p(b), \ord_q(b)\big)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The map $\pi$ of \eqref{eq:crt} is an injective homomorphism, hence an
isomorphism by cardinality, and the order of an element of a direct product is
the lcm of the orders of its components.
\end{proof}

The Legendre symbol $\leg{b}{p}$ is $+1$ if $b$ is a nonzero square modulo $p$
and $-1$ otherwise; the Jacobi symbol for $N = pq$ is
$J(b\mid N) = \leg{b}{p}\leg{b}{q}$.

\section{Layer 1: the coupling is exact at a prime}

\begin{theorem}[Exact residue--order coupling]\label{thm:coupling}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $u$ a unit modulo $p$. Then
\[
  u \text{ is a quadratic residue} \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \ord_p(u) \mid H_p .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides characterise the unique index-$2$ subgroup of the cyclic group
$\units{p}$. Explicitly, $\ord_p(u) \mid H_p$ is equivalent to $u^{H_p} = 1$,
i.e.\ to $u^{(p-1)/2} = 1$, which by Euler's criterion holds exactly when $u$ is
a square. Structurally: writing $g$ for a generator, $u = g^k$ is a square iff
$k$ is even, and $(g^k)^{(p-1)/2} = 1$ iff $(p-1) \mid k(p-1)/2$ iff $k$ is even.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:legendre}
For an odd prime $p$ and a unit $u$: $\leg{u}{p} = 1 \iff \ord_p(u) \mid H_p$.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling} explains the empirical observation that the coupling
verified on $7000$ of $7000$ tested instances: this is not a strong bias but a
theorem with no exceptions. Residues \emph{are} the short-order elements.

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling} does not make order-finding easy. It says the single
bit ``is $\ord_p(u) \mid H_p$?'' is computable in polynomial time at a
\emph{known} prime; it says nothing about the value of $\ord_p(u)$, and nothing
at all about a composite modulus.
\end{remark}

\section{Layer 2, forward direction: residues have short order}

Fix $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ odd primes and $L = \lcmop(H_p, H_q)$.

\begin{definition}[Order class and residue quadrants]
\[
  \mathcal{O}_N = \{\, b \in \units{N} : \ord_N(b) \mid L \,\}, \qquad
  \mathcal{Q}^{\varepsilon\delta}_N = \Big\{\, b : \leg{b}{p} = \varepsilon,\; \leg{b}{q} = \delta \,\Big\},
\]
for $\varepsilon, \delta \in \{\pm 1\}$. We call $\mathcal{Q}^{++}_N$ the
\emph{both-residue quadrant}.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Forward half of the joint law]\label{thm:forward}
$\mathcal{Q}^{++}_N \subseteq \mathcal{O}_N$: if $b$ is a quadratic residue
modulo $p$ and modulo $q$, then $\ord_N(b) \mid L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling} at each prime, $\ord_p(b) \mid H_p \mid L$ and
$\ord_q(b) \mid H_q \mid L$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:ordlcm}, $\ord_N(b)$ is the lcm of
these two, hence divides $L$.
\end{proof}

This inclusion is the source of the observed conditional bias: the $J = +1$
class is $\mathcal{Q}^{++}_N \sqcup \mathcal{Q}^{--}_N$, and the first summand
consists exclusively of elements of order dividing $L \le H_pH_q = \varphi(N)/4$.

\section{Layer 2, converse: the $2$-adic dichotomy}

\subsection{The arithmetic core}

\begin{lemma}[lcm--gcd lattice lemma]\label{lem:lattice}
Let $a, x, y$ be positive integers with $a \mid 2x$,
$a \mid \lcmop(x,y)$ and $v_2(y) \le v_2(x)$. Then $a \mid x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
It suffices to show $\gcd(2x, \lcmop(x,y)) \mid x$, since $a$ divides the
left-hand side. Compare $\ell$-adic valuations. For odd $\ell$,
\[
  \min\big(v_\ell(2x),\, \max(v_\ell(x), v_\ell(y))\big)
  = \min\big(v_\ell(x),\, \max(v_\ell(x), v_\ell(y))\big) = v_\ell(x).
\]
For $\ell = 2$, using $v_2(y) \le v_2(x)$,
\[
  \min\big(1 + v_2(x),\, \max(v_2(x), v_2(y))\big) = \min\big(1+v_2(x),\, v_2(x)\big) = v_2(x).
\]
Hence every valuation of the gcd is at most that of $x$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:lattice} is the pivot of the paper: it isolates the only way the
lift can fail, through a surplus power of $2$.

\subsection{A counterexample when the dial is unbalanced}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of the converse]\label{thm:counterexample}
Suppose $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ and $q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$. Then $b = -1$ satisfies
$\ord_N(b) \mid L$ while $\leg{-1}{p} = -1$. In particular
$\mathcal{O}_N \not\subseteq \mathcal{Q}^{++}_N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\ord_N(-1) = 2$. Since $q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, Lemma~\ref{lem:H} gives
$2 \mid H_q \mid L$, so $-1 \in \mathcal{O}_N$. Since $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$, the
first supplement to quadratic reciprocity makes $-1$ a non-residue modulo $p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$N = 39 = 3 \cdot 13$: here $H_3 = 1$, $H_{13} = 6$, $L = 6$, and the unit
$38 \equiv -1$ has order $2 \mid 6$, yet $38 \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ is a non-residue
modulo $3$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Sufficiency of balance}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp converse under $2$-adic balance]\label{thm:sufficiency}
If $v_2(H_p) = v_2(H_q)$, then for every unit $b$ modulo $N$,
\[
  \ord_N(b) \mid L \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \leg{b}{p} = \leg{b}{q} = 1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $a = \ord_p(b)$. Since $\units{p}$ has order $p - 1 = 2H_p$, we have
$a \mid 2H_p$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:ordlcm}, $a \mid \ord_N(b) \mid L = \lcmop(H_p,H_q)$.
The hypothesis gives $v_2(H_q) \le v_2(H_p)$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:lattice} with
$x = H_p$, $y = H_q$ yields $a \mid H_p$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling} makes
$b$ a residue modulo $p$. The argument at $q$ is symmetric, using
$v_2(H_p) \le v_2(H_q)$ and $\lcmop(H_q,H_p) = L$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Necessity, and the dichotomy}

\begin{lemma}[Existence of a low-order non-residue]\label{lem:lownonres}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $m$ a positive integer with $v_2(H_p) < v_2(m)$.
Then there is a unit $x$ modulo $p$ with $\ord_p(x) \mid m$ and $x$ a
non-residue.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $s = v_2(H_p)$; then $2^{s+1} \mid p-1$ and $2^{s+1} \mid m$. The cyclic
group $\units{p}$ contains an element $x$ of order exactly $2^{s+1}$, so
$\ord_p(x) \mid m$. But $2^{s+1} \nmid H_p$, so $\ord_p(x) \nmid H_p$, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling} makes $x$ a non-residue.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The exact dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ odd primes. Then
\[
  \Big(\forall\, b \in \units{N}:\; \ord_N(b) \mid L \iff \leg{b}{p} = \leg{b}{q} = 1\Big)
  \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad v_2(H_p) = v_2(H_q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:forward} gives one implication for every $b$,
and Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficiency} gives the other under balance.

($\Rightarrow$) Suppose $v_2(H_p) \ne v_2(H_q)$, without loss of generality
$v_2(H_p) < v_2(H_q)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:lownonres} with $m = H_q$ there is a
non-residue $x$ modulo $p$ with $\ord_p(x) \mid H_q$. Using \eqref{eq:crt}, let
$b$ correspond to the pair $(x,1)$; then $\ord_N(b) = \ord_p(x) \mid H_q \mid L$,
while $b$ is a non-residue modulo $p$, so the equivalence fails at $b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The dial and its bottom rung]\label{cor:dial}
By \eqref{eq:v2dial}, $v_2(H_p) = 0$ iff $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$. Hence balance
holds automatically when $p \equiv q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ (the \emph{Blum} case),
and there $\ord_N(b) \mid L \iff b \in \mathcal{Q}^{++}_N$ for every unit.
More generally, balance is the comparison of two integers, $v_2(H_p)$ and
$v_2(H_q)$: a single \emph{dial} whose lowest setting is a condition on $p$ and
$q$ modulo $4$, hence visible in $N \bmod 4$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Layer 2, quantitative: equal quadrants and a blind symbol}

Throughout this section $p \equiv q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:countsquares}
For an odd prime $p$, the squaring endomorphism of $\units{p}$ has kernel
$\{\pm 1\}$ of size $2$, hence image of size $H_p$; there are exactly $H_p$
quadratic residues.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cardunits}
For $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ odd primes,
$\#\units{N} = \varphi(N) = (p-1)(q-1) = 4H_pH_q$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Equinumerous residue quadrants]\label{thm:quadrants}
For each $\varepsilon, \delta \in \{\pm 1\}$,
$\#\mathcal{Q}^{\varepsilon\delta}_N = H_pH_q = \tfrac14 \varphi(N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Under \eqref{eq:crt}, $\mathcal{Q}^{\varepsilon\delta}_N$ corresponds to a
product of a Legendre fibre at $p$ with one at $q$; each has $H_p$
(resp.\ $H_q$) elements by Lemma~\ref{lem:countsquares}, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:cardunits} identifies $H_pH_q$ with $\varphi(N)/4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative joint law on the Blum dial]\label{thm:orderclass}
For $p \equiv q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$,
\[
  \#\mathcal{O}_N \;=\; H_pH_q \;=\; \tfrac14\varphi(N),
  \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad 4 \cdot \#\mathcal{O}_N = \#\units{N}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:dial}, $\mathcal{O}_N = \mathcal{Q}^{++}_N$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:quadrants}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The Jacobi symbol is blind to the order class]\label{thm:blind}
For $p \equiv q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$,
\[
  J(1\mid N) = J(-1\mid N) = +1, \qquad \ord_N(1) \mid L, \qquad \ord_N(-1) \nmid L .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$N = pq \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, so $J(-1\mid N) = (-1)^{(N-1)/2} = +1$, and trivially
$J(1\mid N) = +1$. Now $\ord_N(1) = 1$ divides everything, while $\ord_N(-1) = 2$
and $L = \lcmop(H_p,H_q)$ is odd because $H_p$ and $H_q$ are odd by
Lemma~\ref{lem:H}; hence $2 \nmid L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Interpretation]
The Jacobi symbol is the \emph{product} of the two Legendre symbols, so it
merges $\mathcal{Q}^{++}_N$ with $\mathcal{Q}^{--}_N$. The order class
$\mathcal{O}_N$ is exactly $\mathcal{Q}^{++}_N$, i.e.\ half of the $J = +1$
class. Theorem~\ref{thm:blind} exhibits an explicit witnessing pair:
$1 \in \mathcal{Q}^{++}_N$ and $-1 \in \mathcal{Q}^{--}_N$ are
indistinguishable to the symbol and distinguished by the order. The order
statistic is strictly finer, and the extra information is exactly what would
require knowing $p$ and $q$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Source of the conditional bias]\label{cor:bias}
Conditioning on $J = +1$ selects a class of size $\varphi(N)/2$, exactly half of
which ($\varphi(N)/4$ elements, namely $\mathcal{Q}^{++}_N$) has order dividing
the odd number $L$. Conditioning on $J = -1$ selects
$\mathcal{Q}^{+-}_N \sqcup \mathcal{Q}^{-+}_N$, disjoint from $\mathcal{O}_N$.
Hence $\Ex[\ord_N \mid J = +1] < \Ex[\ord_N \mid J = -1]$ is structurally
forced, which is precisely the observed bias.
\end{corollary}

\begin{observation}\label{obs:threequarters}
For every tested $N = pq$ with $p \equiv q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ --- including
$21, 33, 77, 133, 209, 437, 713$ --- the ratio in Corollary~\ref{cor:bias}
equals exactly $3/4$. This is an empirical observation, not a theorem of this
paper, and it is a natural target for future work.
\end{observation}

\section{Layer 3: the joint law does not determine the factorisation}

\subsection{Computable presentation}
For $b$ coprime to $N$, put
$\ord^{\mathrm c}(N,b) = \min\{ k \in [1,N] : b^k \equiv 1 \bmod N \}$, which
agrees with $\ord_N(b)$ because the order of a unit is at most $\varphi(N) < N$.
For an odd prime $p$ with $p \nmid b$, Euler's criterion gives the computable
symbol $\mathrm{qr}(p,b) = +1$ if $b^{(p-1)/2} \equiv 1 \bmod p$ and $-1$
otherwise, and $\mathrm{qr}(p,b) = \leg{b}{p}$. Consequently, for $N = pq$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:comp}
  \Lw(N) = \big\{\!\!\big\{\, \big(\ord^{\mathrm c}(N,b),\; \mathrm{qr}(p,b)\,\mathrm{qr}(q,b)\big) : 0 \le b < N,\ \gcd(b,N) = 1 \,\big\}\!\!\big\},
\end{equation}
which reduces the equality of two joint laws to a finite exhaustive computation.

\subsection{The collision}

\begin{theorem}[Two coprime semiprimes with the same joint law]\label{thm:collision}
$\Lw(35) = \Lw(39)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both moduli have $\varphi(35) = \varphi(39) = 24$ units. Evaluating
\eqref{eq:comp} at $35 = 5\cdot 7$ and at $39 = 3\cdot 13$ yields the same
multiset:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccccc}
\toprule
$(\ord, J)$ & $(1,+)$ & $(2,+)$ & $(2,-)$ & $(3,+)$ & $(4,+)$ & $(4,-)$ & $(6,+)$ & $(6,-)$ & $(12,+)$ & $(12,-)$ \\
\midrule
in $\Lw(35)$ & 1 & 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 & 4 & 4 \\
in $\Lw(39)$ & 1 & 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 & 4 & 4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Both rows sum to $24$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:condcollision}
For each $\varepsilon \in \{\pm 1\}$ the conditional laws
$\Lw(35)\!\restriction_{J=\varepsilon}$ and $\Lw(39)\!\restriction_{J=\varepsilon}$
coincide; in particular the conditional order sums agree ($77$ at $J = +1$ and
$84$ at $J = -1$ for both moduli), hence so do all conditional moments.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Abstract barrier]\label{thm:abstractbarrier}
Let $N_1, N_2$ be coprime with $\Lw(N_1) = \Lw(N_2)$, and let $F$ be any
function from multisets of pairs to natural numbers. Then it is impossible that
$F(\Lw(N_i)) > 1$ and $F(\Lw(N_i)) \mid N_i$ for both $i = 1, 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $d = F(\Lw(N_1)) = F(\Lw(N_2))$, well defined since the arguments are
equal. Then $d \mid \gcd(N_1,N_2) = 1$, so $d = 1$, contradicting $d > 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Concrete barrier]\label{thm:concretebarrier}
There is no function $F$ from joint laws to natural numbers such that for every
$N \in \{35, 39\}$ the value $F(\Lw(N))$ is a nontrivial divisor of $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:collision}, $\gcd(35,39) = 1$, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:abstractbarrier}.
\end{proof}

Two features deserve emphasis. The statement is \emph{unconditional}: no
unproved hypothesis, no asymptotic regime, no restriction on the computational
power of $F$, which may be arbitrary and even non-computable. And it is
\emph{maximal}: every conditional statistic of the pair (order, Jacobi symbol)
is a function of $\Lw$, so the theorem simultaneously refutes every attack in
this family, including ones not yet proposed.

\subsection{Why collisions are structural}

\begin{theorem}[Transport of the joint law]\label{thm:transport}
Let $N_1, N_2$ be odd moduli and let
$e : \units{N_1} \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ } \units{N_2}$ be a group isomorphism with
$J(e(u) \mid N_2) = J(u \mid N_1)$ for all $u$. Then $\Lw(N_1) = \Lw(N_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A group isomorphism preserves element orders, $\ord(e(u)) = \ord(u)$. Combined
with the hypothesis on symbols, $u \mapsto e(u)$ carries each pair
$(\ord(u), J(u\mid N_1))$ to the identical pair
$(\ord(e(u)), J(e(u)\mid N_2))$; since $e$ is a bijection it induces an equality
of multisets.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The coarse invariant]
Theorem~\ref{thm:transport} says $\Lw$ is not really a function of $N$ but of
the isomorphism class of the pair $\big(\units{N}, J(\cdot \mid N)\big)$: an
abelian group of order $\varphi(N)$ together with a quadratic character on it.
For $N = pq$ the group is $\Z_{p-1} \times \Z_{q-1}$. The number of isomorphism
classes of such pairs with $\varphi(N) \le Y$ grows far more slowly than the
number of semiprimes with $\varphi(N) \le Y$, so collisions are forced by
pigeonhole in the quotient category rather than being numerical accidents.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
Our collision is of this type:
$\units{35} \cong \Z_4 \times \Z_6$ and $\units{39} \cong \Z_2 \times \Z_{12}$,
both isomorphic to $\Z_2 \times \Z_4 \times \Z_3$, and the quadratic character
corresponds under this identification.
\end{example}

\begin{observation}[Collisions are common already at small scale]\label{obs:collisions}
An exhaustive search over the $73$ semiprimes $N = pq < 400$ with $p < q$ odd
primes finds only $62$ distinct joint laws. Ten of those laws are shared by two
or more pairwise coprime moduli, each yielding an independent instance of
Theorem~\ref{thm:abstractbarrier}: for example $\{35,39\}$, $\{77,93\}$,
$\{95,111\}$, $\{143,155,183\}$, $\{161,201\}$, $\{203,215\}$,
$\{247,259\}$, $\{299,335\}$ and $\{319,355\}$. The triple
$\{143,155,183\}$ is instructive: the unit groups
$\Z_{10}\times\Z_{12}$, $\Z_4\times\Z_{30}$ and $\Z_2\times\Z_{60}$ are all
the same abelian group of order $120$. This is exactly the pigeonhole behaviour
predicted by Theorem~\ref{thm:transport}.
\end{observation}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Order-class test at a known prime}
Given an odd prime $p$ and a unit $b$: set $H \leftarrow (p-1)/2$, compute
$r \leftarrow b^H \bmod p$ by fast exponentiation, and return $r = 1$. Cost
$O(\log p)$ modular multiplications. Correctness is
Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling}; the output answers simultaneously ``is $b$ a
square?'' and ``does $\ord_p(b)$ divide $H_p$?''.

\subsection{Two-adic balance test}
Given odd primes $p \ne q$: set $H_p \leftarrow (p-1)/2$,
$H_q \leftarrow (q-1)/2$ and return $v_2(H_p) = v_2(H_q)$. Cost $O(\log N)$ bit
operations; correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}. The test returns true
whenever $p \equiv q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$. Crucially it requires $p$ and $q$ and is
therefore unavailable to an adversary holding only $N$.

\subsection{Exhaustive joint-law computation and collision search}
For each $b$ coprime to $N = pq$, compute $\ord_p(b)$ and $\ord_q(b)$ by
factoring $p-1$ and $q-1$ and peeling prime powers, set
$o \leftarrow \lcmop(\ord_p(b), \ord_q(b))$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:ordlcm}) and
$j \leftarrow \mathrm{qr}(p,b)\,\mathrm{qr}(q,b)$, and insert $(o,j)$ into the
multiset. Cost $O(\varphi(N)\log^2 N)$ modular operations, versus $O(N^2)$ for
naive definition-chasing. Collision search then compares canonically sorted
multisets over candidate moduli in $O(\varphi(N)\log \varphi(N))$ per pair. The
order computation is where circularity bites: an adversary who can run it on a
large $N$ has already won.

\subsection{Permutation test for factor dependence}
Given triples $(p_i, q_i, r_i)$ with $r_i$ the bias ratio for $N_i$, and a
covariate $f$ (e.g.\ $f(p,q) = |p-q|$), set $x_i = f(p_i,q_i)$, compute
$\mathrm{obs} = |\mathrm{Pearson}(x, r)|$, then for $T$ trials permute $r$
uniformly at random and record $|\mathrm{Pearson}(x, r')|$; report $\mathrm{obs}$
against the null $95$th percentile. Cost $O(Tn)$. This is the procedure behind
the reported observed correlations $\le 0.31$ against null percentiles
$0.34$--$0.41$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Three independent obstructions}
The order $\times$ Jacobi statistic fails as a factoring route for three
logically independent reasons, each sufficient alone.

\emph{(i) It is a residue dial.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} the whole
structure is controlled by $v_2(H_p) \overset{?}{=} v_2(H_q)$, whose bottom rung
is a condition on $N \bmod 4$; there the quadrants are exactly equal
(Theorem~\ref{thm:orderclass}), leaving no excess to exploit.

\emph{(ii) It is circular.} Evaluating the law requires component orders, and
computing multiplicative order modulo a composite is, so far as is known, no
easier than factoring.

\emph{(iii) It collides.} Theorem~\ref{thm:concretebarrier} removes the last
hope: even granting an oracle for the law, no function of it can factor, because
two coprime moduli already share the law; Theorem~\ref{thm:transport} shows this
is generic rather than lucky.

\subsection{What the empirical bias actually was}
The observed conditional bias --- real, reproducible, ratio strictly below $1$
--- is fully explained by Corollary~\ref{cor:bias} as an artefact of the coset
structure, with no residual factor dependence. This is the characteristic
signature of a closed attack surface: the signal exists, the mechanism is
understood, and the mechanism is factor-oblivious.

\subsection{Positive content}
Independently of any cryptographic reading: Euler's criterion is a statement
about \emph{cycle lengths} (Theorem~\ref{thm:coupling}); the failure of this to
lift to composites is measured by a single $2$-adic invariant
(Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}), a clean local--global obstruction concentrated at
one prime; the Blum integers acquire a new characterisation as exactly the
semiprimes on the lowest rung of the dial, where the order test is an exact test
for double residuosity; and the joint law is an invariant of the pair (unit
group, quadratic character) (Theorem~\ref{thm:transport}), whose fibres pose a
well-posed question in multiplicative number theory.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}
The barrier concerns the joint law of the pair (order, Jacobi symbol) over all
units. It says nothing about attacks using partial information about $p$ or $q$
(Coppersmith-type methods), which fall outside the hint-free classical regime,
nor about quantum order-finding, which uses the order of a single element in a
fundamentally different way. The collision is exhibited at $N = 35, 39$, which
suffices for the barrier as a universal statement over $F$, but leaves open the
density of collisions at cryptographic sizes.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Collision density]
The number of $N \le X$ that are products of two distinct odd primes and admit a
coprime semiprime $N' \le X^{1+o(1)}$ with $\Lw(N) = \Lw(N')$ is $(1-o(1))$
times the number of semiprimes up to $X$.
\end{conjecture}
\noindent\emph{Key insight.} The joint law only remembers the pair (abelian
group $\Z_{p-1}\times\Z_{q-1}$, quadratic character), and the number of such
isomorphism classes with $\varphi(N) \le Y$ grows far more slowly than the
number of semiprimes: a pigeonhole in a quotient category, not in the integers.
Theorem~\ref{thm:transport} makes the transport mechanism a theorem, so the
conjecture reduces to counting isomorphism classes of pairs (group, character),
a tractable problem of Erd\H{o}s--Pomerance type on fibres of $\varphi$.
Observation~\ref{obs:collisions} already shows the deficiency of distinct laws
setting in below $400$.

\begin{conjecture}[Universality of the dial]
Let $\chi$ be a Dirichlet character mod $N = pq$ of order $\ell$ and consider
the statistic $u \mapsto (\ord(u), \chi(u))$. Then the analogue of
Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} holds with $v_2$ replaced by $v_\ell$: the test
$\ord(u) \mid \lcmop\!\big(\tfrac{p-1}{\ell}, \tfrac{q-1}{\ell}\big)$ is
equivalent to ``$\chi$ trivial at both components'' if and only if
$v_\ell\!\big(\tfrac{p-1}{\ell}\big) = v_\ell\!\big(\tfrac{q-1}{\ell}\big)$.
\end{conjecture}
\noindent\emph{Key insight.} The dichotomy proof used nothing about
quadraticity beyond the lattice identity
$\gcd(2x, \lcmop(x,y)) = x \iff v_2(y) \le v_2(x)$, and the same identity holds
prime-by-prime for any $\ell$. The arithmetic core is already isolated as
Lemma~\ref{lem:lattice}.

\begin{conjecture}[Hardness of the inverse problem]
There is a polynomial-time reduction from factoring semiprimes to the problem:
given the multiset $\Lw(N)$ as an explicit list, together with $N$, output a
nontrivial factor of $N$. Conversely, given the factorisation the law is
computable in polynomial time.
\end{conjecture}
\noindent\emph{Key insight.} The law's $+1$-fibre determines the multiset of lcm
values of pairs of divisors of $(p-1)/2$ and $(q-1)/2$, a divisor-lattice
tomography problem whose difficulty can be pinned to the hardness of recovering
$p-1$ from $\varphi(N)$-type data.

\medskip

\noindent Two further targets. First, the exact-$3/4$ phenomenon of
Observation~\ref{obs:threequarters}: proving or refuting it would upgrade
Corollary~\ref{cor:bias} to an exact identity. Second, the remaining cell of the
combination grid --- residue data paired with spectral data --- is predicted to
collapse in the same way and for the same three reasons; a corresponding
collision theorem there would close the grid.

\section{Conclusion}

The order $\times$ Jacobi joint law of a semiprime is a genuinely beautiful
object. The coupling that generates it is exact at every odd prime --- quadratic
residues are precisely the elements of order dividing $(p-1)/2$ --- and its lift
to a semiprime fails or succeeds according to a single, crisply identified
$2$-adic comparison. On the Blum rung $p \equiv q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ the lift is
exact, the residue quadrants are exactly equal quarters, and the Jacobi symbol
is provably blind to which order class a unit occupies.

It is also, as a route to factorisation, definitively closed. The conditional
bias is real but structural. The law is invariant under Jacobi-preserving
isomorphism of unit groups, hence a function of an object far coarser than the
factorisation, and two explicit coprime moduli --- $35$ and $39$ --- already
share it. From that single collision follows an unconditional impossibility: no
function whatsoever of the order $\times$ Jacobi joint law can return a
nontrivial divisor of its modulus.

